Date: Fri, 19 Nov 1993 09:50:17 -0600
From: cpresson@ingr.com (Craig Presson)
Subject: How Voluntary Socialism is Disproven Rationally

In <9311191458.aa01027@genie.genie.slhs.udel.edu>, nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu writes:
|> I'm not sure that Tim has disproven all possible forms of socialism
|> by bringing up the calculation problem, since after the Singularity,
|> people will be able to calculate much better. Is there a proof
|> that the complexity of what people **want and will do** about it is
|> *always* going to be greater than their calculational ability?
** emphases mine, * emphases Nancy's

Yes -- I am such a proof. I do not **want** my economic needs attended
to by someone's computer program, no matter how complex, subtle,
feature-rich, and generally warm-fuzzy-perfect it is, and I would be
**willing** to take major risks to sabotage such a system. Nor am I
alone.

|> Also, would a society in which everyone voluntarily lives in communes
|> that own property and compete/cooperate with each other count as
|> free market or socialist? I think that a society like that would
|> avoid the calculation problem, at least to a fairly large extent.

I think you've described a free-market system with economic units
larger than the individual, which is analogous to what we have today
with families, so it does not avoid the calculation problem. People
should be free to try any voluntary alternative-living system they
like, until they decide to try something else.

The free market is optimal, it is better than any socialist utopia, it
is moral, and rational. It is not what we use because we don't know
how to set up utopia. It is not our second choice. If I could say it
any plainer I would.
