Date: Fri, 19 Nov 93 16:11:55 GMT
From: jrk@sys.uea.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: How Voluntary Socialism is Disproven Rationally

Nancy Lebovitz writes:
>I'm not sure that Tim has disproven all possible forms of socialism
>by bringing up the calculation problem, since after the Singularity,
>people will be able to calculate much better. Is there a proof
>that the complexity of what people want and will do about it is
>*always* going to be greater than their calculational ability?

I am very dubious of people claiming irrefutable proofs of statements about
the real world, especially on such woolly matters as human behaviour.  I
don't have any concrete bone to pick with Tim Starr's argument though.  I
wouldn't be surprised if some similar mathematico-logical flim-flam (sorry,
but that really is how I see it) could be used to establish the
unsolvability of the calculation problem.

A more fundamental objection is that the "calculation problem" has no
solution, because it is a meaningless problem.  What is an optimal
allocation of resources?  Who decides?  Markets do not solve this problem,
they ignore it.  I consider this a virtue, not a defect, because the
"problem" does not exist.

>Also, would a society in which everyone voluntarily lives in communes
>that own property and compete/cooperate with each other count as
>free market or socialist? I think that a society like that would
>avoid the calculation problem, at least to a fairly large extent.

If pigs flew, would we call them aeroplanes or birds?

