From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Getting paid for Net surfing

First, quoting a rate of $100/hour for net surfing makes good press,
but I really doubt that it's the going rate.  If so, look for that
going rate to plummet as the zillions of net freaks smell opportunity.

I suspect that there is room for a number of highly-paid highly-skilled
net surfers just as there is in any area.  Anybody wanting to do this,
though -- be good at it.  Be damned good at it.  Have quick access to
all archived USENET posts.  Have a very good net connection.  Have
a large personal database of information sources (where to go; 'archie'
won't suffice!).  Have a steady working relationship with all of
the major commercial information providers.  Understand copyright
law.  Have an encyclopedic knowledge of government and company FTP
sites, including the detailed reasons it's in their own interest
to provide them.  Etc.  This is work, not play.

That said, I do agree with Nick that Extropians could do more to
support each other in this kind of practical venture.  In terms of raw
intelligence (whatever that means), net savvy, technical knowledge,
and so on, we have a pretty strong group here.  If anybody is interested
in building such net knowledge, I'd be willing to participate in
a VirtSem on this topic... let's see what kinds of information about
effective net surfing we can collect.  As I am embarked on a mildly
related path myself at present, it is certainly in my best interest to
learn as much as I can about net.resources.

R. David Murray:
 
> AnswerMan would be a clearing house.

Well, beware of building clearing houses without sufficient stuff
to clear.  None of this fancy infrastructure is really required.
All that's required is a point man.  Pay world.std.com $5/month
for an e-mail mailbox -- perhaps "answers@world.std.com" [or whatever
commercial provider you prefer; you get the point].  Get the word
out:

  Service announcement:  Do you have a need for information, of
  any kind?  Send a description of your needs to answers@world.std.com
  for a free quote.  We will research anything.  You don't pay for
  anything but results.

If desirable, get a list of people who are interested in working
as part of the "Answers Consortium".  I hereby join (completely
serious, let's do it, why not?).  You don't need newsgroup
hierarchies and software automation to start this.  It could
be started this afternoon, very easily.

> Clearly there are lots of implementation details here.

But none that are relevant to starting small, which is clearly
the best option.

> For example, one could do small transactions in Thornes

Nick already does this.  True, so far at least he only has a
set catalog of information available, but he could change that
very easily.

derek

