Kaspar Suter writes:

> Tim May answers Michael Kelly's "Size of intergrated circuit" question:
> 
> >Usually measured in mm or in mils (.001 inch), more natural units.
> >Modern microprocessors are about 500 mils on a side (.5 inch). Or
> >about 10-15 mm.
> 
> In integrated circuit design you use microns (micron= um = micrometer)
> Design rules are specified in microns. Companies sell you circuits
> talking about X micrometer technology.

No, Kaspar is conflating feature size with the overall size of the
chip, which is as I recall what Michael Kelly was asking about. I
spent 12 years at Intel, presently the leading chip company, and can
affirm that feature sizes are measured in microns (as in ".6 micron")
and chip dimensions are measured in millimeters or mils, depending.

(That all linear dimensions are convertible is of course true, a point
I cited.)

> "mils" are the collapse of the imperial measurement system. Having nothing
> adequate to go below the inch (besides fractions as 3/8" and so on...) they
> jump into metric and leave one leg in imperial. You can call an inch or a feet
> a more natural unit than cm or m. But you are not taking advantage of metric
> just by inventing mils. How many square inches has a 500x500 mils chip? How
> many inches are in a mile? How many fl oz in a barrel? Got it?
> Only Spielberg gets dinosaurs back to live :-) . 

No, I guess I didn't "get it." I'll inform Intel and Motorola that
their use of a non-metric system is unacceptable. I agree that metric
is slightly easier to compute in, but either system is easy enough to
work in---and one can't change the entire industry as the flick of a
keystroke. 

Kaspar's tone of voice ("Got it?") leaves something to be desired, to
phrase this in the passive voice that Amara was just writing about. 

(Oh, and "How many square inches has a 500x500 mils chip?" is,
trivially, a quarter square inch.)

--Tim May

