From: jrk@sys.uea.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: Drugs, control, identity

My favorite topic, and one which my favourite author, Greg Egan, has just
written yet another story about ("Chaff", in issue 78 of Interzone, a
British SF magazine).

SPOILER WARNING: the text below reveals much of the plot of "Chaff".

What does it mean to claim control over something?  To be able to change
it, or to be able to prevent it from changing?  The control that Kennita
values over her self seems to be only the latter.  And indeed, if one's
self can change one's self, who is in control?  Who is changing what?

Everything that you do changes who you are, whether it is taking LSD,
taking a job, meeting a stranger, or walking the dog.  Getting up in the
morning changes you; not getting up changes you.

Derek Zahn commented that a faithful upload might in time come to do things
markedly different from what the "real" you would have done, but it is not
so: the different possible "real" yous could end up doing equally different
things.  Contrary to the standard pop-science example of chaos, the beat of
the butterfly's wings does not cause the tornado three weeks later.  There
are possible histories that contain both the wing-beat and the tornado,
other histories that contain only either one, others that contain neither. 
Short of the Omega Point, the future history of the system is not
computable by anything other than the system itself.  Thus it will be, I
suspect, with uploads.  One cannot predict exactly what an upload will do,
only the sort of things it might do.  What it actually does may eventually
be drastically different from what one particular "you" would have done in
a similar situation, yet that does not in itself make it an unfaithful
representation of "you".

But what of that supposed bedrock, our unchangeable "primate heritage",
"reptilian brain", or whatever metaphorical entity you prefer, over which
some like to see all of civilisation as a paper-thin veneer?  Perhaps there
is such a thing, for now; but if you cannot change it, it is not you who
control it, but it which controls you.  To change it would be a true
liberation, though like all freedom, it brings its own dangers.

"Chaff" postulates the invention of a neural modification which can by
deliberate decision of the user reprogram one's brain in any way one
chooses.  The title comes from a line in Conrad's "Heart of Darkness":
"less than chaff in a breeze".  Conrad is speaking of "civilised"
behaviour, comparing it to the immutable realities of our animal past, our
heart of darkness.  In Egan's story, that animal heritage too, and all of
human nature, becomes less than chaff in a breeze; there is nowhere any
longer any firm, immutable ground of identity whatsoever.  (I wonder how
Detweiler would react to the story?)


I will quote the ending.  The narrator is a professional assassin, sent to
dispatch the inventor of the "Grey Knights" (the biotechnological McGuffin
of the story), but is instead captured and infected with them himself.

   "Grey Knights moved only if you willed them to move.  All I had to do in
order to remain unscathed was to choose that fate.  To wish to be only
exactly who I was.
...
    I had to feel nothing -- and wish to feel nothing -- and keep on making
that choice, again and again.
    Or everything that I was would disintegrate like a house of sand, and
blow away.
    One of the guards belched in the darkness, then spat.
    The night stretched out ahead of me, like a river which had lost its way."

