Date: Fri, 19 Nov 93 10:32:18 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: The LP is Dead

(And I don't mean because CDs have taken over. I've changed the name
of this thread from "Still..." to something a bit more descriptive.)

Craig Presson wrote:

> In <199311182348.PAA24922@mail.netcom.com>, Timothy C. May writes:
> |> I don't belong to the Libertarian Party partly because I refuse to
> |> sign their silly pledge against use of force.
> 
> It's a pledge against _initiation_ of force. It's also an excuse for
> yet another round of factional infighting this year (if it hadn't been
> that, they'd have found something ...).

Yes, this was a slip on my part, as I'm aware they use the phrase
"initiation of force."

My gripe is that the term "initiation" is too vague to be of use. Am I
initiating force, for example, if I blow up the local Post Office
monopoly? (Even if it's empty, or contains only government employees?)

Am I initiating force to use the services of HitNet to take out
government agents and politicians?

The LP is silent on these issues. Or at least I can find no serious
discussion in my LP propaganda. And most LP'ers I know (I sometimes go
to the Santa Cruz LP meetings, though not recently) have no clue on
what's coming vis-a-vis crypto anarchy.

(Yes, I've tried to explain it to them--being in a rather good
position to explain crypto anarchy to them--but their eyes glaze over
whenever I mention digital money and the like. One member of my local
group was adamant that such things were impossible....a few months
later he read about "RSA" for apparently the first time and he began
to have a glimmer that I wasn't making all this stuff up.)

To my mind, the LP is irrelevant. In 20+ years of existence, they've
had 4350+ chances to elect a Representative, 400+ chances to elect a
Senator, and of course 6 chances to elect a President. They've never
come close. And I doubt they ever will. The public remains too hostile
to anarcho-capitalist ideas.

What _will_ change thing is technology, as it has so often in the
past. The printing press, the automobile, the radio, etc.

World-wide black market and anonymous systems, and all the other
aspects of crypto anarchy, will make the ballot box a moot issue.

--Tim May

