Subject: LANG: E-prime and General Semantic Swamp

Perry Metzger writes:
>Indeed, expressing "The map is not the territory", a very GS cliche,
>is difficult in E-Prime because you couldn't express the underlying
>fallacy in E-Prime to begin with.

Strict E-Prime speakers can say: "The map and the territory always differ".

Another variation on strict E-Prime has been proposed by Robert Anton
Wilson: he forbids "is", but allows "is not", on the grounds that the
semantic reactions[*] evoked by the latter are of a healthy character
quite unlike the unsanity[*] of the positive "is".

[*] These are technical terms of GS, which I'd have to get out my copy of
S&S (the full version, 4th edition) to explain.

